WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.559
One tripod position.

00:00:01.559 --> 00:00:03.778
Two fisheye lenses, back to back.

00:00:04.158 --> 00:00:07.083
The practitioners' rule: don't solve poses on these.

00:00:07.083 --> 00:00:08.545
Stitch a panorama first.

00:00:11.168 --> 00:00:12.130
Hi, I'm Nadir.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:15.980
I took one public 360 capture with a laser scan of the

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:17.584
room, and tested that rule.

00:00:21.617 --> 00:00:25.355
Draw a small ring of directions and push it toward the ceiling.

00:00:25.355 --> 00:00:27.223
On the panorama it smears sideways.

00:00:27.643 --> 00:00:30.945
Cut the sphere into cube faces and the ring stays round, until

00:00:30.945 --> 00:00:32.045
it crosses an edge.

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:36.093
On the two fisheye circles it stays round almost everywhere, except on

00:00:36.093 --> 00:00:38.210
the seam, where each lens sees half.

00:00:40.325 --> 00:00:43.734
FIORD ships no panorama, so I stitched my own, and masked

00:00:43.734 --> 00:00:44.973
the seam in red.

00:00:45.353 --> 00:00:46.883
It came out upside down.

00:00:46.883 --> 00:00:50.860
The frames are stored that way, and my tripod mask hid the ceiling.

00:00:52.308 --> 00:00:53.709
Dense, every arm registered.

00:00:53.709 --> 00:00:56.511
So I thinned the capture to fourteen positions.

00:00:56.931 --> 00:00:58.600
Loose cube faces fell apart.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:02.273
Three models, and not one position with all its faces together.

00:01:02.693 --> 00:01:07.161
The panorama as one camera, or cube faces with the rig: all fourteen whole.

00:01:07.581 --> 00:01:11.487
The surprise: the raw fisheye, with no rig at all, kept thirteen.

00:01:11.487 --> 00:01:14.742
Each lens sees past ninety degrees, so the two overlap.

00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:20.430
Even dense, a rigless cube position's five faces land a median one

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:23.403
point eight centimetres apart, and half a degree off.

00:01:25.775 --> 00:01:28.430
Then I trained the splat at four degrees of colour.

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:31.615
Three more degrees buy a third of a decibel, for three point

00:01:31.615 --> 00:01:32.677
six times the file.

00:01:33.097 --> 00:01:36.456
Spend those bytes on more splats instead, and it does not help either.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.777
Structure from motion gives a shape with no size.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:45.952
Fitted to the scan, the camera sits one point six five metres up.

00:01:45.952 --> 00:01:46.818
That's a tripod.

00:01:47.238 --> 00:01:50.164
The scan's walls are six point seven three metres apart.

00:01:51.838 --> 00:01:54.597
And each scanned wall is a band a few millimetres thick.

00:01:54.597 --> 00:01:56.854
That is the bar a splat has to meet.

00:01:58.492 --> 00:02:00.610
A LiDAR scanner sells five millimetres.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:04.474
But that is drift between targets, not the noise on one pipe.

00:02:06.267 --> 00:02:09.191
Solve on the panorama, or give the mapper the rig.

00:02:09.191 --> 00:02:10.945
Never hand it loose cube faces.

00:02:14.233 --> 00:02:18.087
Loose cube faces break, extra colour degrees barely pay, and check which

00:02:18.087 --> 00:02:19.693
way up your frames are.

00:02:19.693 --> 00:02:20.656
Thanks for watching!

00:02:21.076 --> 00:02:23.099
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:23.099 --> 00:02:23.677
I'm Nadir.

00:02:23.677 --> 00:02:23.966
Bye!
